# tests/tcyr/stream_end.tcyr — did the stream actually end? (0.7.0)
#
# `chitra_image_seen_iend` exists to answer one question: did the encoded stream
# end the way its format says it should? PNG answered it honestly from 0.2.0.
# JPEG, BMP and GIF hardcoded `1` — so on three formats of four, the single
# accessor a consumer could use to detect an incomplete stream was a constant.
#
# That matters because chitra deliberately DECODES incomplete streams rather
# than rejecting them, exactly as libjpeg does: a JPEG truncated inside its
# entropy data comes back as fabricated zero-padded MCUs, and a GIF whose LZW
# ends early comes back with its tail filled by palette entry 0 at full alpha.
# Both are reasonable decodes. Both were indistinguishable from a complete one.
#
# These cells pin the signal for every format, in both directions — a suite
# that only checked the truncated cases would pass with the field hardcoded
# the other way.
#
# Fixture provenance: see tests/tcyr/jpeg_noninterleaved.tcyr for the corpus
# recipe. The GIF short fixture is built by shortening the first LZW sub-block
# and splicing in the end-of-blocks terminator, which leaves a STRUCTURALLY
# VALID file — ImageMagick reads it without complaint — whose pixel data simply
# stops early. Plain truncation mostly rejects instead, so it would not
# exercise this path at all.
include "src/lib.cyr"
include "lib/assert.cyr"

# 24x16 baseline 4:2:0 JPEG, complete, byte sum 223,824.
fn fx_se_jpeg(len_out): i64 {
    var b = alloc(689);
    store64(len_out, 689);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < 689) {
        store8(b + i, 0);
        i = i + 1;
